\begin_layout Standard
Quantum mechanics provides a framework of physical and mathematical principles through which we seek to understand the physical nature of the world in which we live.
 These principles are fundamental and are presumed to apply to all physical systems.
 They embody fundamental physical and philosophical questions that can be abstracted from any particular physical system and studied independently of the specific systems in which they may be manifested.
 It is this abstract formulation of these principles that constitutes quantum mechanics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Some historical background
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Planck’s Black Body Theory (1900):

\series default
 In an attempt to understand the form of the spectrum of the electromagnetic radiation emitted by an object in thermal equilibrium at some temperature (a black body),
 Planck was forced to propose that the atoms making up the object absorbed and emitted light of (angular) frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 in multiples of a fundamental unit of energy,
 or quantum of energy,
 
\begin_inset Formula $E=\hbar\omega$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hbar=h/2\pi$
\end_inset

 and where 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is a new fundamental constant of nature,
 Planck’s constant,
 
\begin_inset Formula $h=6.622\times10^{-34}Js^{-1}$
\end_inset

 though the name Planck’s constant is now often applied to 
\begin_inset Formula $\hbar$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Einstein’s Light Quanta (1905):

\series default
 Although Planck believed that the rule for the absorption and emission of light in quanta applied only to black body radiation,
 and was a property of the atoms,
 rather than the radiation,
 Einstein saw it as a property of electromagnetic radiation,
 whether it was black body radiation or of any other origin.In particular,
 in his work on the photoelectric effect,
 he proposed that light of frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 was made up of particles of energy 
\begin_inset Formula $\hbar\omega$
\end_inset

,
 now known as photons,
 which could be only absorbed or emitted in their entirety.So light,
 a form of wave motion,
 had been given a particle character.Much later,
 in 1922,
 the particle nature of light was quite explicitly confirmed in the light scattering experiments of Compton.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
De Broglie’s Hypothesis
\series default
 (1924) Inspired by Einstein’s picture of light,
 a form of wave motion,
 as also behaving in some circumstances as if it was made up of particles,
 and inspired also by the success of the Bohr model of the hydrogen atom,
 de Broglie was lead,
 by purely aesthetic arguments to make a radical proposal.If light waves of frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 can behave under some circumstances like a collection of particles of energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E=\hbar\omega$
\end_inset

,
 then by symmetry,
 a massive particle of energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

,
 an electron say,
 should behave under some circumstances like a wave of frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega=E/\hbar$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But a defining characteristic of a wave is its wavelength.
 For a photon,
 the wavelength of the associated wave is 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=c/f$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $f=\omega/2\pi$
\end_inset

 .
 So what is it for a massive particle?
 From Einstein’s theory of relativity,
 which showed that the energy of a photon (moving freely in empty space) is related to its momentum 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $E=pc$
\end_inset

,
 it follows that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E=\hbar\omega=\hbar2\pi f=\frac{\hbar2\pi}{\lambda}c=\frac{h}{\lambda}c
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So 
\begin_inset Formula $p=h/\lambda$
\end_inset

.
 This equation then gave the wavelength of the photon in terms of its momentum,
 but it is also an expression that contains nothing that is specific to a photon.So De Broglie assumed that this relationship applied to all free particles,
 whether they were photons or electrons or anything else,
 and so arrived at the pair of equations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\frac{E}{h},\qquad\lambda=\frac{h}{p}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which gave the frequency and wavelength of the waves that were to be associated with a free particle of kinetic energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 and momentum 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
 But now,
 given that particles can exhibit wave like properties,
 the natural question that arises is:
 what is doing the ‘waving’ ?
 Further,
 as wave motion is usually describable in terms of some kind of wave equation,
 it is then also natural to ask what the wave equation is for these de Broglie waves.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The latter question turned out to be much easier to answer than the first – these waves satisfy the famous Schr¨ odinger wave equation.But what these waves are is still,
 largely speaking,
 an incompletely answered question.Nevertheless,
 it is possible to say a considerable amount about what these waves tell us about the particle.
 It is this topic to which we now turn.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Harmonic Wave Function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the basis of de Broglie’s hypothesis,
 there is associated with a particle of energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 and momentum 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

,
 a wave of frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 and wavelength 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 given by the de Broglie relations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\frac{E}{h},\qquad\lambda=\frac{h}{p}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It more usual to work in terms of the angular frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega=2\pi f$
\end_inset

 and wave number 
\begin_inset Formula $k=2\pi/\lambda$
\end_inset

 so that the de Broglie relations become
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\omega=E/\hbar\qquad k=p/\hbar
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With this in mind,
 we are in a position to make a reasonable guess at a mathematical expression for the wave associated with the particle.The possibilities include (in one dimension)
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Recall that when we solved the Schrödinger equation for the free particle,
 we obtained two of these possibilities.
\end_layout

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Psi\left(x,t\right)=A\sin\left(kx-\omega t\right),\quad Ae^{-i\left(kx+\omega t\right)}+Ae^{i\left(kx-\omega t\right)},\dots
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At this stage,
 we have no idea what the quantity 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\left(x,t\right)$
\end_inset

 represents physically.It is given the name the wave function,
 and in this particular case we will use the term harmonic wave function to describe any trigonometric wave function of the kind listed above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to understand what information may be contained in the wave function,
 which will lead us to gaining a physical understanding of what it might represent,
 we will turn things around briefly and look at what we can learn about the properties of a particle if we know what its wave function is.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First,
 given that the wave has frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 and wave number 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 then it is straightforward to calculate the phase velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{p}$
\end_inset

 of the wave:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{p}=\frac{\omega}{k}=\frac{\hbar\omega}{\hbar k}=\frac{E}{p}=\frac{\frac{1}{2}mv^{2}}{mv}=\frac{1}{2}v
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 given the frequency and wave number of a wave function,
 we can determine the speed of the particle from the phase velocity of its wave function,
 
\begin_inset Formula $v=2v_{p}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We could also try to learn from the wave function the position of the particle.However,
 the wave function above tells us nothing about where the particle is to be found in space.We can make this statement because this wave function is the same everywhere i.e.
 there is nothing whatsoever to distinguish 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi$
\end_inset

 at one point in space from any other.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 this particular wave function gives no information on the whereabouts of the particle with which it is associated.So from a harmonic wave function it is possible to learn how fast a particle is moving,
 but not what the position is of the particle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Wave Packets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From what was said above about a wave function that was constant throughout all space,
 it would seem that a wave function can only convey information on the position of the particle if the wave function did not have the same amplitude throughout all space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In fact,
 since what we mean by a particle is a physical object that is confined to a highly localized region in space,
 ideally a point,
 it would be intuitively appealing to be able to devise a wave function that is zero or nearly so everywhere in space except for one localized region.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is in fact possible to construct,
 from the harmonic wave functions,
 a wave function which has this property.To show how this is done,
 we first consider what happens if we combine together two harmonic waves of very close frequency.
 The resultis well-known:
 a ‘beat note’ is produced,
 i.e.
 periodically in space the waves add together in phase to produce a local maximum,
 while midway in between the waves will be totally out of phase and hence will destructively interfere.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each localized maximum is known as a wave packet,
 so what is produced is a series of wave packets.Now suppose we add together a large number of harmonic waves with wave numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1},k_{2},k_{3},\dots$
\end_inset

 all lying in the range:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\overline{k}-\Delta k<k_{n}<\overline{k}+\Delta k
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
around a mean value 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{k}$
\end_inset

,
 i.
 e.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\Psi\left(x,t\right) & =A\left(k_{1}\right)\cos\left(k_{1}x-\omega t\right)+A\left(k_{2}\right)\cos\left(k_{2}x-\omega t\right)+\dots\\
 & =\sum_{n}A\left(k_{n}\right)\cos\left(k_{n}x-\omega t\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $A\left(k_{n}\right)$
\end_inset

 is a function peaked about the mean value 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{k}$
\end_inset

 with a full width at half maximum 
\begin_inset Formula $2\Delta k$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
That is,
 
\begin_inset Formula $A\left(k_{n}\right)$
\end_inset

 has a peak at 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{k}$
\end_inset

 and assumes half of its maximum value at a distance 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta k$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{k}$
\end_inset

,
 so that its full width at half maximum is 
\begin_inset Formula $2\Delta k$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 What is found is that in the limit in which the sum becomes an integral:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\Psi\left(x,t\right) & =\int^{+\infty}_{-\infty}A\left(k\right)\cos\left(kx-\omega t\right)dk
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
all the waves interfere constructively to produce only a single beat note (in effect,
 the ‘beat notes’ or wave packets are infinitely far apart).In other words,
 the wave function so constructed is found to have essentially zero amplitude everywhere except for a single localized region in space,
 over a region of width 
\begin_inset Formula $2\Delta x$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.
 the wave function 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\left(x,t\right)$
\end_inset

 in this case takes the form of a single wave packet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This wave packet is clearly particle-like in that its region of significant magnitude is confined to a localized region in space.Moreover,
 this wave packet is constructed out of a group of waves with an average wave number 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{k}$
\end_inset

,
 and so these waves could be associated in some sense with a particle of momentum 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}=h\overline{k}$
\end_inset

.
 If this were true,
 then the wave packet would be expected to move with a velocity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}/m$
\end_inset

.This is in fact found to be the case,
 as the following calculation shows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because a wave packet is made up of individual waves which themselves are moving,
 though not with the same speed,
 the wave packet itself will move (and spread as well).
 The speed with which the wave packet moves is given by its group velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{g}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{g}=\left(\frac{d\omega}{dk}\right)_{k=\overline{k}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is the speed of the maximum of the wave packet i.e.
 it is the speed of the point on the wave packet where all the waves are in phase.Calculating the group velocity requires determining the relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 known as a dispersion relation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This dispersion relation is obtained from
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E=\frac{1}{2}mv^{2}=\frac{p^{2}}{2m}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Substituting in the de Broglie relations (
\begin_inset Formula $f=\frac{E}{h},\lambda=\frac{h}{p}$
\end_inset

) gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
hf & =\frac{h^{2}}{\lambda^{2}}\frac{1}{2m}\\
\frac{h}{2\pi}\omega & =\left(\frac{h}{2\pi}\right)^{2}\frac{k^{2}}{2m}\\
\hbar\omega & =\frac{\hbar^{2}k^{2}}{2m}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where we also use:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega=2\pi f$
\end_inset

 and wave number 
\begin_inset Formula $k=2\pi/\lambda$
\end_inset

.
 From which follows the dispersion relation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\omega=\frac{\hbar k^{2}}{2m}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The group velocity of the wave packet is then
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{g}=\left(\frac{d\omega}{dk}\right)_{k=\overline{k}}=\frac{\hbar k}{m}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}=\hbar\overline{k}$
\end_inset

,
 this becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{g}=\overline{p}/m$
\end_inset

 .i.e.the packet is indeed moving with the velocity of a particle of momentum 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}$
\end_inset

,
 as suspected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is a result of some significance,
 i.e.
 we have constructed a wave function of the form of a wave packet which is particle-like in nature.But unfortunately this is done at a cost.
 We had to combine together harmonic wave functions 
\begin_inset Formula $\cos\left(kx-\omega t\right)$
\end_inset

 with a range of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 values 
\begin_inset Formula $2\Delta k$
\end_inset

 to produce a wave packet which has a spread in space of size 
\begin_inset Formula $2\Delta k$
\end_inset

.The two ranges of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 are not unrelated – their connection is embodied in an important result known as the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The wave packet constructed in the previous section obviously has properties that are reminiscent of a particle,
 but it is not entirely particle-like – the wave function is non-zero over a region in space of size 
\begin_inset Formula $2\Delta x$
\end_inset

.In the absence of any better way of relating the wave function to the position of the atom,
 it is intuitively appealing to suppose that where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\left(x,t\right)$
\end_inset

 has its greatest amplitude is where the particle is most likely to be found,
 i.e the particle is to be found somewhere in a region of size 
\begin_inset Formula $2\Delta x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
More than that,
 however,
 we have seen that to construct this wavepacket,
 harmonic waves having 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 values in the range 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(k-\Delta k,k+\Delta k\right)$
\end_inset

 were adding together.These ranges 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta k$
\end_inset

 are related by the bandwidth theorem,
 which applies when adding together harmonic waves,
 which tell us that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta x\Delta k\gtrsim1
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using 
\begin_inset Formula $p=\hbar k$
\end_inset

,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p=\hbar\Delta k$
\end_inset

 so that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta x\Delta p\ge\hbar
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
[A more rigorous derivation,
 based on a more precise definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta k$
\end_inset

 leads to
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta x\Delta p\ge\frac{\hbar}{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A closer look at this result is warranted.A wave packet that has a significant amplitude within a range 
\begin_inset Formula $2\Delta x$
\end_inset

 was constructed from harmonic wave functions which represent a range of momenta 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}-\Delta p$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}+\Delta p$
\end_inset

.We can say then say that the particle is likely to be found somewhere in the region 
\begin_inset Formula $2\Delta x$
\end_inset

,
 and given that wave functions representing a range of possible momenta were used to form this wave packet,
 we could also say that the momentum of the particle will have a value in the range 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}-\Delta p$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}+\Delta p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The quantities 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p$
\end_inset

 are known as uncertainties for reasons that will become increasingly apparent,
 and the relation above is known as the Heisenberg uncertainty relation for position and momentum.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Postulates of quantum mechanics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Notation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To move forward,
 we need to know the postulates
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A postulate is a proposition assumed to be true within a theory,
 serving as a starting point for the construction of its concepts and results.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 of quantum mechanics.
 The first point to note is that they can be formulated in different ways from one book to another.
 We will use Professor Jonas Maziero's material (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "maziero"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 We need to begin,
 then,
 with some comments on Dirac notation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this notation,
 we write a vector as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|v\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 called a ket.
 Taking the conjugate transpose of this vector (or adjoint),
 we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle v\right|=\left|v\right\rangle ^{\dagger}=\left(\left|v\right\rangle ^{T}\right)^{*}$
\end_inset

,
 which is called a bra.
 If we represent the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|v\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 in an orthonormal basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|b_{j}\right\rangle \right\} $
\end_inset

,
 we can write it as a linear combination of the basis vectors:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|v\right\rangle =\sum_{i}\left\langle b_{i}|v\right\rangle \left|b_{i}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
 In matrix representation,
 we have
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left|v\right\rangle =\left[\begin{array}{c}
\left\langle b_{1}|v\right\rangle \\
\vdots\\
\left\langle b_{n}|v\right\rangle 
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The product of a bra and a ket is called the inner product and results in a scalar:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle w|v\right\rangle =\left|w\right\rangle ^{\dagger}\left|v\right\rangle =\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\left\langle b_{1}|w\right\rangle ^{*} & \dots & \left\langle b_{n}|w\right\rangle ^{*}\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
\left\langle b_{1}|v\right\rangle \\
\vdots\\
\left\langle b_{n}|v\right\rangle 
\end{array}\right]=\sum^{n}_{j=1}\left\langle b_{j}|w\right\rangle ^{*}\left\langle b_{j}|v\right\rangle 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

On the other hand,
 the product of a ket and a bra is called the outer product and results in an operator,
 whose matrix representation is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left|v\right\rangle \left\langle w\right|=\left|v\right\rangle \left|w\right\rangle ^{\dagger}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\left\langle b_{1}|v\right\rangle \\
\vdots\\
\left\langle b_{n}|v\right\rangle 
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\left\langle b_{1}|w\right\rangle ^{*} & \dots & \left\langle b_{n}|w\right\rangle ^{*}\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\left\langle b_{1}|v\right\rangle \left\langle b_{1}|w\right\rangle ^{*} & \dots & \left\langle b_{1}|v\right\rangle \left\langle b_{n}|w\right\rangle ^{*}\\
\vdots & \vdots & \vdots\\
\left\langle b_{n}|v\right\rangle \left\langle b_{1}|w\right\rangle ^{*} & \dots & \left\langle b_{n}|v\right\rangle \left\langle b_{n}|w\right\rangle ^{*}
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With this in mind,
 we can now move on to the postulates of quantum mechanics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
State Postulate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
In every physical theory,
 we seek to describe the possible states of the system of interest,
 the changes in these states (temporal evolution),
 and the observable quantities and their measurements.
 For example,
 in Newtonian Mechanics (NM),
 the state of a system at a certain instant of time is specified by the values of position and velocity relative to a certain inertial reference frame,
 and its temporal evolution is given by Newton's second law.
 The observables are essentially the positions,
 from which we obtain the velocities using the time parameter.
 It is implicit in NM that the values of the observables are determined even before they are measured,
 and that all these observables can have well-defined values at a given instant of time.
 Next,
 we will see the postulates of QM for describing states,
 changes in these states,
 observables,
 and their measurements.
 We will see that the measurement part differs greatly from that of NM,
 mainly because of the existence of observables that cannot have well-defined values at the same time (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "maziero"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The state of a quantum system is described by a normalized vector in a Hilbert space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

.
 For simplicity,
 we will consider 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 as a vector space equipped with an inner product function 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle \cdot|\cdot\right\rangle :\mathcal{H}\rightarrow\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore,
 a Hilbert space is a complete complex vector space.
 To understand what it means to be complete,
 we first need to understand the concept of a Cauchy sequence.
 A Cauchy sequence is a sequence whose elements become arbitrarily close to one another as we advance through the sequence.
 A complete metric space
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A metric space is,
 essentially,
 a set of objects in which we define a way of measuring the distance between two objects.
 In the case of a vector space with an inner product,
 the distance can be defined from the norm induced by the inner product.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 is one in which every Cauchy sequence converges to a limit that also belongs to the space itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By system,
 we refer to a degree of freedom
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A degree of freedom corresponds to an independent physical variable that can vary and that can be used to characterize the state of a system.
 For example,
 the position and spin of a particle correspond to different degrees of freedom.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 For example,
 the spin of an electron is a system,
 another system would be its position,
 and so on.
 When we consider two or more degrees of freedom as a single entity,
 we call it a composite system,
 and if we divide the composite system into subsystems,
 its state is described by a normalized vector in a Hilbert space obtained by taking the tensor product of the individual spaces:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}=\mathcal{H}_{a}\otimes\mathcal{H}_{b}\otimes\dots
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Measurement Postulate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Observable (measurable) quantities are represented by linear Hermitian operators,
 
\begin_inset Formula $O=O^{\dagger}$
\end_inset

,
 defined on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

.
 When we measure an observable,
 we will obtain one of its possible values,
 which are given by the eigenvalues of the Hermitian operator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike classical mechanics (CM),
 quantum mechanics (QM),
 in general,
 does not allow us to predict with certainty the outcome of a measurement.
 What QM provides us with are the probabilities of obtaining the different outcomes,
 calculated by means of the Born rule:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P\left(o|\psi\right)=\left|\left\langle \psi|o\right\rangle \right|^{2}=\left|\left\langle o|\psi\right\rangle \right|^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is the probability of obtaining the value 
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

 when measuring the observable 
\begin_inset Formula $O$
\end_inset

 of a system that is in the state 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\psi\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 immediately before the measurement,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|o\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 is the eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $O$
\end_inset

 corresponding to the eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $O=O^{\dagger}$
\end_inset

 are defined on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

.
 When we measure an observable,
 we obtain one of its possible values,
 which are represented by the eigenvalues of the Hermitian operator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before moving forward,
 we can stop to reflect for a moment.
 When we were working with classical mechanics and Newton's law,
 one of the observables of a particle was its position,
 and Newton's law directly described how this quantity varied in time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 when we approach quantum mechanics,
 the Schrödinger equation describes how the wave function varies in time (and space).
 With Dirac notation,
 as we will see when studying the dynamics postulate,
 we have a mathematical description of how the state vector of the system varies in time.
 However,
 the state vector is not itself an observable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Even temporarily ignoring the probabilistic aspect,
 we use the state vector to calculate quantities associated with observables.
 This already constitutes a fundamental difference between the classical and quantum descriptions of systems.
 But if we calculate scalar quantities associated with observables from the state vector (or the wave function) of the system,
 what is the physical meaning of the state vector itself?
 Does it even have a direct physical meaning?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is one of the points on which different interpretations of quantum mechanics diverge.
 There is still no consensus on which interpretation provides the correct description of physical reality,
 although some interpretations may be ruled out or considered problematic in light of certain theoretical arguments and experimental results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Likewise,
 different interpretations diverge on the question of whether all relevant physical information about a system is necessarily contained in its state vector.
 We will explore different interpretations later on.
 For now,
 it is enough to emphasize that the physical meaning of the state vector and the status of the information it contains are profound interpretational questions on which there is no consensus.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Repeatability of measurements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 there is one more detail that,
 although it is not explicitly contained in the postulates we have presented so far,
 is significantly different when we are in the quantum domain:
 performing a measurement affects the system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When we observe the position of a soccer ball that moves according to classical mechanics,
 we can,
 in principle,
 make this observation without significantly altering the state of the ball.
 We can therefore consider that the observation was performed approximately “externally” to the system itself,
 leaving it practically unchanged.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the quantum domain,
 however,
 the situation is different.
 When we perform a measurement,
 the system interacts with the measurement apparatus and,
 in general,
 its state is significantly altered.
 One consequence of this is that,
 if we measure the same observable twice immediately in succession,
 the result of the second measurement will necessarily be equal to that obtained in the first.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Indeed,
 suppose that the first measurement of the observable (
\begin_inset Formula $O$
\end_inset

) produced the result (
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

).
 After this measurement,
 the state of the system becomes a state associated with the result (
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

).
 In the case where the eigenvalue (
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

) is non-degenerate
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A non-degenerate eigenvalue is one to which a single eigenvector corresponds,
 up to a global phase,
 that is,
 a complex phase factor 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{i\phi}$
\end_inset

 that multiplies the state vector and does not affect the measurement outcomes,
 since 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{i\phi}\left(e^{i\phi}\right)^{*}=1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 we can write
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P\left(o|\psi\right)=1,\quad\rightarrow\left|\psi\right\rangle =e^{i\phi}\left|o\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A second immediately subsequent measurement of the same observable will therefore once again produce the result (
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that,
 theoretically,
 before the measurement is performed,
 quantum mechanics provides us only with the probabilities associated with the possible outcomes.
 In the laboratory,
 however,
 an individual realization of the measurement necessarily produces a definite outcome.
 This leads us to another fundamental question:
 the probabilistic nature of quantum mechanics.
 Its probabilistic predictions concern the frequencies and distributions of outcomes obtained from ensembles of systems prepared in equivalent ways and subjected to the same experimental procedure,
 and they do not,
 in general,
 determine which specific outcome will be obtained in an individual realization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before the measurement is performed,
 we have only the probabilities associated with the different values we may obtain.
 In this situation,
 in what state is the system?
 When dealing with classical mechanics,
 deterministic dynamics allows us,
 in principle,
 to determine the state of a system at an earlier instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t'<t$
\end_inset

 by knowing its state at an instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Can we extend this line of reasoning to quantum mechanics?
 Is the probabilistic character of the theory a fundamental feature of nature,
 or merely an expression of our ignorance about the state of the system?
 Again,
 we have no conclusive answers to this question,
 and different interpretations of quantum mechanics offer different answers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And finally,
 still related to the probabilistic nature of measurements,
 misguided interpretations often support the idea that quantum mechanics is a scientific formulation that naturally incorporates the possibility of free will.
 However,
 the probabilistic character of the theory,
 by itself,
 provides no support for this conclusion and does not make quantum mechanics compatible with free will.
 We can discuss this misconception in greater detail when we address the dynamics postulate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Quantum incompatibility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 be two Hermitian operators defined on a finite-dimensional Hilbert space of dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

.
 By the spectral theorem,
 we can write them in their respective spectral decompositions as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
A= & \sum^{d}_{j=1}a_{j}\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{j}\right| & B= & \sum^{d}_{j=1}b_{j}\left|b_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle b_{j}\right|
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{j}$
\end_inset

 are the respective eigenvalues,
 and the sets
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|a_{j}\right\rangle \right\} ^{d}_{j=1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|b_{j}\right\rangle \right\} ^{d}_{j=1}$
\end_inset

 form orthonormal bases of eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 respectively,
 that is,
 they diagonalize these operators,
 so that:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
A\left|a_{j}\right\rangle  & =a_{j}\left|a_{j}\right\rangle  & B\left|b_{j}\right\rangle = & b_{j}\left|b_{j}\right\rangle 
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming that the two operators share the same basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|a_{j}\right\rangle =\left|b_{j}\right\rangle \coloneqq\left|j\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 then,
 with the commutator defined as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[A,B\right]=AB-BA$
\end_inset

,
 we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\left[A,B\right] & =\left(\sum^{d}_{j=1}a_{j}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|\right)\left(\sum^{d}_{k=1}b_{k}\left|k\right\rangle \left\langle k\right|\right)-\left(\sum^{d}_{k=1}b_{k}\left|k\right\rangle \left\langle k\right|\right)\left(\sum^{d}_{j=1}a_{j}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|\right)\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j,k=1}a_{j}b_{k}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle j|k\right\rangle \left\langle k\right|-\sum^{d}_{j,k=1}b_{k}a_{j}\left|k\right\rangle \left\langle k|j\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j,k=1}a_{j}b_{k}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle k\right|\delta_{jk}-\sum^{d}_{j,k=1}b_{k}a_{j}\left|k\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|\delta_{jk}\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}\left(a_{j}b_{j}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|-b_{j}a_{j}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|\right)\\
 & =0
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
0=\left[A,B\right]=AB-BA\Longrightarrow AB=BA
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 we have that if the operators share the same set of eigenvectors,
 they commute.
 And to prove that if they commute,
 they share the same basis of eigenvectors:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
A\left(B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \right) & =\left(AB\right)\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \\
 & =B\left(A\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \right)\\
 & =B\left(a_{j}\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \right)\\
 & =a_{j}B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle 
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is,
 in this case,
 we must have that 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|a_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 belong to the eigenspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 corresponding to the eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is non-degenerate,
 its eigenspace has dimension 1.
 Therefore,
 any eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is linearly dependent on 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|a_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 also belongs to this eigenspace,
 we necessarily have 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle =b_{j}\left|a_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is degenerate,
 that is,
 if there are two or more linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the same eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 then there exists a subspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 generated by the eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 corresponding to 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 It is a little more complex,
 but it is the same idea applied to a subspace of dimension greater than 1.
 To understand this,
 we will base our discussion on another source (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "MIT804_CommonEigenbases"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the degenerate case,
 we then have more vectors associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Let us call this subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 and,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle \right\} ^{d_{j}}_{k=1}$
\end_inset

 being any basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $A\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle =a_{j}\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
 Now,
 since 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 commute,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \in\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 that is,
 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 transforms vectors from 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 into vectors that also belong to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 In particular,
 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 transforms an eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 into a vector that remains in the same eigenspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle \neq0$
\end_inset

,
 this vector is also an eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore,
 each 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 can be written as a linear combination of the basis vectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 we can consider the action of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 “internally” within the subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is Hermitian,
 its restriction to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 is also Hermitian.
 And since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 has finite dimension (and is Hermitian),
 we can diagonalize this restriction of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 in an orthonormal basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
 there exists a basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 which we can denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle \right\} $
\end_inset

,
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle =b_{j}\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
 Since this new basis remains a basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 its vectors continue to be eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 the eigenvectors are simultaneously eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 Repeating this procedure for each eigenspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 we obtain an orthonormal basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 consisting of common eigenvectors of the two operators.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In other words,
 if the Hermitian operators 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 commute,
 then they share the same orthonormal basis of eigenvectors.
 To better understand this proof,
 we can represent the operator 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 as a matrix in the basis of eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 Using the completeness relation,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{I}=\sum_{j}\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{j}\right|$
\end_inset

,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $B=\mathbb{I}B\mathbb{I}=\sum_{j,k}\left(\left\langle a_{j}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle \right)\left(\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{k}\right|\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 the elements of the matrix representing 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 in this basis are 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{a_{j},a_{k}}=\left\langle a_{j}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 or in matrix form:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\left\langle a_{1}\left|B\right|a_{1}\right\rangle  & \left\langle a_{1}\left|B\right|a_{2}\right\rangle  & \dots\\
\left\langle a_{2}\left|B\right|a_{1}\right\rangle  & \left\langle a_{2}\left|B\right|a_{2}\right\rangle  & \dots\\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then we can think of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 as a block-diagonal matrix:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
B_{1} & 0 & 0\\
0 & B_{2} & 0\\
0 & 0 & \ddots
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where each 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}$
\end_inset

 is a submatrix representing the internal action of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 acting on the subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 If the eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is non-degenerate,
 then the block 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}$
\end_inset

 is just a 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times1$
\end_inset

 matrix.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is degenerate,
 then the block 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}$
\end_inset

 has dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{j}\times d_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is Hermitian,
 each block 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}$
\end_inset

 is also a Hermitian matrix and,
 therefore,
 can be diagonalized.
 Thus,
 we can diagonalize 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 within each subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 obtaining a basis of eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 that are also eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 we prove that if and only if two operators 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 commute,
 then they share a common basis of eigenvectors.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center

\series bold
Example:
 Beginning
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us consider the operator 
\begin_inset Formula $A=\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 2
\end{pmatrix}.$
\end_inset

 To verify that it is a Hermitian matrix,
 we can verify its conjugate transpose
\begin_inset Formula $A^{\dagger}=A.$
\end_inset

 Its eigenvalues are 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ 1,1,2\right\} ,$
\end_inset

and the corresponding eigenvectors (each column is an eigenvector) are 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\left|a_{1}\right\rangle ,\left|a_{2}\right\rangle ,\left|a_{3}\right\rangle \right)=\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}.$
\end_inset

 We have then that eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 is degenerate.
 We can reconstruct matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 by spectral decomposition:
 
\begin_inset Formula $A=\sum^{3}_{n=1}a_{n}\left|a_{n}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{n}\right|.$
\end_inset

 We have now defined a Hermitian matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $B=\begin{pmatrix}3 & 1 & 0\\
1 & 4 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 5
\end{pmatrix}.$
\end_inset

 We can verify that they commute,
 that is,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[A,B\right]=AB-BA=0$
\end_inset

.
 In the simplest case,
 we then have a subspace of only one dimension for eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

.
 We can see that 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a_{3}\right\rangle =5\left|a_{3}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now for eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

,
 we have a two-dimensional subspace formed by the vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|a_{1}\right\rangle ,\left|a_{2}\right\rangle \right\} $
\end_inset

.
 All non-null vectors in this subspace continue to be eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 with the same eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

,
 Any non-null vector that is a linear combination of them,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|u\right\rangle =a\left|a_{1}\right\rangle +b\left|a_{2}\right\rangle ,$
\end_inset

 is also an eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 In fact,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\left|u\right\rangle =A\left(a\left|a_{1}\right\rangle +b\left|a_{2}\right\rangle \right)=aA\left|a_{1}\right\rangle +bA\left|a_{2}\right\rangle =a\left(1\cdot\left|a_{1}\right\rangle \right)+b\left(1\cdot\left|a_{2}\right\rangle \right)=a\left|a_{1}\right\rangle +b\left|a_{2}\right\rangle =1\cdot\left|u\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now applying 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 to the first eigenvector,
 we can see that it is a linear combination of the other two vectors in the subspace and is not a 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 eigenvector:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B\left|a_{1}\right\rangle =\begin{pmatrix}3 & 1 & 0\\
1 & 4 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 5
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1\\
0\\
0
\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}3\\
1\\
0
\end{pmatrix}=3\left|a_{1}\right\rangle +\left|a_{2}\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is,
 even though these two eigenvectors associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 are a basis for this subspace,
 they do not form a common eigenvector basis.
 But we can build one.
 Since all non-null vectors in this subspace are eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 we only need to find those who are also eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 In this two-dimensional space,
 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

's action on the vectors of the space is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{1}=\begin{pmatrix}3 & 1\\
1 & 4
\end{pmatrix}.$
\end_inset

 Its eigenvalues are 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ 2.38,4.62\right\} $
\end_inset

 and the corresponding eigenvectors (each column is an eigenvector) are 
\begin_inset Formula $\begin{pmatrix}-0.85 & -0.53\\
0.53 & -0.85
\end{pmatrix}.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Combining with the previous results,
 we can have a new basis given by the eigenvectors (where each column corresponds to a vector of the new basis)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(\left|b_{1}\right\rangle ,\left|b_{2}\right\rangle ,\left|b_{3}\right\rangle \right)=\begin{pmatrix}-0.85 & -0.53 & 0\\
0.53 & -0.85 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can test by applying 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to the vectors of the new basis:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
A\left|b_{1}\right\rangle  & =\left|b_{1}\right\rangle  & \begin{pmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 2
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}-0.85\\
0.53\\
0
\end{pmatrix} & =\begin{pmatrix}-0.85\\
0.53\\
0
\end{pmatrix}\\
A\left|b_{2}\right\rangle  & =\left|b_{2}\right\rangle  & \begin{pmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 2
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}-0.53\\
-0.85\\
0
\end{pmatrix} & =\begin{pmatrix}-0.53\\
-0.85\\
0
\end{pmatrix}\\
A\left|b_{3}\right\rangle  & =2\left|b_{3}\right\rangle  & \begin{pmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 2
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}0\\
0\\
1
\end{pmatrix} & =2\begin{pmatrix}0\\
0\\
1
\end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can test by applying 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 to the vectors of the new basis:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
B\left|b_{1}\right\rangle  & =2.38\left|b_{1}\right\rangle  & \begin{pmatrix}3 & 1 & 0\\
1 & 4 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 5
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}-0.85\\
0.53\\
0
\end{pmatrix} & =2.38\begin{pmatrix}-0.85\\
0.53\\
0
\end{pmatrix}\\
B\left|b_{2}\right\rangle  & =4.62\left|b_{2}\right\rangle  & \begin{pmatrix}3 & 1 & 0\\
1 & 4 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 5
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}-0.53\\
-0.85\\
0
\end{pmatrix} & =4.62\begin{pmatrix}-0.53\\
-0.85\\
0
\end{pmatrix}\\
B\left|b_{3}\right\rangle  & =5\left|b_{3}\right\rangle  & \begin{pmatrix}3 & 1 & 0\\
1 & 4 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 5
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}0\\
0\\
1
\end{pmatrix} & =5\begin{pmatrix}0\\
0\\
1
\end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center

\series bold
Example:
 End
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In summary,
 when we have a non-degenerate eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 the situation is very simple,
 since in this case,
 the only eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 up to a multiplicative factor,
 is automatically also an eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is degenerate,
 its eigenvectors form a subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 corresponding to the eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 in which every nonzero vector is also an eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 commute,
 applying 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 to any vector in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 results in another vector belonging to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 it is sufficient to find,
 within this subspace,
 the vectors that are also eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This possibility is guaranteed by the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is Hermitian:
 its action restricted to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 can be represented by a Hermitian submatrix,
 which can be diagonalized by an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The fact that a matrix is Hermitian guarantees that we can choose,
 in the space on which it acts,
 an orthonormal basis formed by its eigenvectors (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "DelftSpectralTheorem"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "on this page"
target "https://interactivetextbooks.tudelft.nl/mqp-v2/operators.html"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.)
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
 we can choose an orthonormal basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 formed by eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 Since all vectors in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 are also eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 these vectors are common eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Repeating this procedure for each eigenspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 we obtain an orthonormal basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 formed by common eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the proof of this theorem,
 we know that if two observables 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 of a system do not commute,
 they cannot share all eigenvectors.
 If,
 by measuring the observable 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 we prepare the system in a state that is not an eigenvector of the observable 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 this vector must necessarily be a linear combination of the eigenvectors of with two or more nonzero probability amplitudes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center

\series bold
Example:
 Beginning
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose we have the operators 
\begin_inset Formula $A=\begin{pmatrix}0 & 1\\
1 & 0
\end{pmatrix}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B=\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0\\
0 & -1
\end{pmatrix}.$
\end_inset

 We can verify that they do not commute,
 since 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
[A,B]=AB-BA=\begin{pmatrix}0 & -2\\
2 & 0
\end{pmatrix}\neq0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the operator 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 the eigenvalues are 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ +1,-1\right\} $
\end_inset

 with corresponding normalized eigenvectors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
|a_{+1}\rangle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{pmatrix}1\\
1
\end{pmatrix},\qquad|a_{-1}\rangle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{pmatrix}-1\\
1
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose,
 for example,
 that after measuring 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 we obtain the value 
\begin_inset Formula $a=+1$
\end_inset

.
 The system is then in the corresponding eigenstate,
 
\begin_inset Formula $|\psi\rangle=|a_{+1}\rangle.$
\end_inset

 Now consider measuring 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 The eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 are also 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ +1,-1\right\} $
\end_inset

 with corresponding eigenvectors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
|b_{+1}\rangle=\begin{pmatrix}1\\
0
\end{pmatrix},\qquad|b_{-1}\rangle=\begin{pmatrix}0\\
1
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The probability of obtaining a particular eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{n}$
\end_inset

 is given by the squared magnitude of the inner product between the corresponding eigenvector and the state of the system:
 
\begin_inset Formula $P(b_{n})=|\langle b_{n}|\psi\rangle|^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
P(b_{+1})=|\langle b_{+1}|a_{+1}\rangle|^{2}=\frac{1}{2}\\
P(b_{-1})=|\langle b_{-1}|a_{-1}\rangle|^{2}=\frac{1}{2}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 there is a 
\begin_inset Formula $50\%$
\end_inset

 probability of observing 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 and a 
\begin_inset Formula $50\%$
\end_inset

 probability of observing 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

.
 In other words,
 since 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 do not commute and,
 in this example,
 do not share any eigenvectors,
 after measuring one of the observables,
 the outcome of a subsequent measurement of the other is intrinsically indeterminate within this mathematical formalism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center

\series bold
Example:
 End
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Uncertainty Relation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here we will use the quantum kinematic formalism to obtain general mathematical relations that provide limitations on the product or sum of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

uncertainties
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (standard deviations) of two observables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Heisenberg-Robertson-Schrödinger Uncertainty Relation (HRSUR)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The HRSUR :
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{Var}\left(A\right)\text{Var}\left(B\right)\ge(\text{CovQ}\left(A,B\right))^{2}+2^{-2}|\langle[A,B]\rangle|^{2},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with the variance defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{Var}(A)=\langle(A-\langle A\rangle)^{2}\rangle.$
\end_inset

 The quantities on the right-hand side of the inequality will be defined below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To prove the HRSUR,
 we will use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality (CSI) as one of the main tools:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\langle\psi|\psi\rangle\langle\phi|\phi\rangle\ge|\langle\psi|\phi\rangle|^{2}=\langle\psi|\phi\rangle\langle\phi|\psi\rangle,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with equality obtained if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $|\psi\rangle$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $|\phi\rangle$
\end_inset

 are collinear (
\begin_inset Formula $|\psi\rangle\propto|\phi\rangle$
\end_inset

).
 This result is valid for any pair of vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $|\psi\rangle,|\phi\rangle$
\end_inset

 in a Hilbert space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}.$
\end_inset

 Consider 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 to be two observables of a physical system prepared in the state 
\begin_inset Formula $|\xi\rangle$
\end_inset

.
 We will use
\end_layout

