\begin_layout Standard
Here we are in a position to engage in some further discussion.
 Heisenberg's uncertainty relation is constantly the subject of misunderstandings.
 The first concerns the status of the relation itself.
 Although it is traditionally called an uncertainty principle,
 it does not need to be postulated as a principle.
 As we have seen,
 Heisenberg's relation can be obtained as a particular case of the Heisenberg-Robertson-Schrödinger Uncertainty Relation,
 which,
 in turn,
 can be derived from the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality,
 together with the mathematical properties of observables represented by operators in a Hilbert space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second point is that it is incorrect to interpret the uncertainty relation as stating that a particle 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

does not have a definite position and momentum
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

we cannot measure position and momentum in the same preparation
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 The first statement is a possible interpretation,
 but it is not the mathematical content of the relation,
 whereas the second is simply incorrect.
 The uncertainty relation does not say that it is impossible to obtain position and momentum results in the same preparation;
 it establishes a relationship between the variances of the results of these measurements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In other words,
 we can repeatedly prepare the system in the same state and perform position and momentum measurements.
 From the results,
 we can determine the respective distributions and,
 consequently,
 their variances.
 The uncertainty relation establishes that the product of these variances —
 or,
 more precisely,
 of the standard deviations —
 must satisfy a given relation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This contrasts with classical mechanics.
 We can imagine,
 for example,
 a particle at rest at a perfectly determined position.
 Ignoring experimental limitations,
 it would be possible to conceive of a classical state in which position and velocity were simultaneously well defined,
 for example,
 a particle at rest at the origin (
\begin_inset Formula $x=0,v=0$
\end_inset

).
 In this case,
 if we repeated the measurements,
 we would always obtain the same values and,
 therefore,
 we would have 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{x}=\sigma_{v}=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Dynamics Postulate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Changes of states in QM are described by a unitary transformation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
|\psi_{t}\rangle=U_{t}|\psi_{0}\rangle,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{t}U^{\dagger}_{t}=U_{t}U^{\dagger}_{t}=\mathbb{I}.
\]

\end_inset

The time-evolution operator being obtained from the Schrödinger equation:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t}=H_{t}U_{t},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with the initial condition:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{0}=\mathbb{I},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the Hamiltonian operator of the system at the instant of time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $\hbar$
\end_inset

 is Planck's constant divided by 
\begin_inset Formula $2\pi$
\end_inset

 and we use the notation 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{t}=\frac{\partial}{\partial_{t}}$
\end_inset

.
 The two equations above are equivalent to the Schrödinger equation for the state 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\partial_{t}|\psi_{t}\rangle=H_{t}|\psi_{t}\rangle.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We verify this result as follows:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
i\hbar\partial_{t}|\psi_{t}\rangle & =i\hbar\partial_{t}(U_{t}|\psi_{0}\rangle)\\
 & =(i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t})|\psi_{0}\rangle\\
 & =H_{t}U_{t}|\psi_{0}\rangle\\
 & =H_{t}|\psi_{t}\rangle.
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center

\series bold
Example:
 Beginning
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{t}=H$
\end_inset

,
 in this type of situation—
i.e.,
 when the Hamiltonian is time-independent—
we can verify that the following general form holds:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
U_{t}=e^{-iHt/\hbar}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We will do this first using the spectral decomposition of the Hamiltonian,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
H=\sum_{j}E_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|,
\]

\end_inset

and applying the operator function:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t} & =i\hbar\partial_{t}\exp\left(-\frac{iHt}{\hbar}\right)\\
 & =i\hbar\partial_{t}\exp\left(\sum_{j}\left(-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}\right)|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\right)
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here we should pause to understand the mathematics.
 We have seen that for any Hermitian operator 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(T)=\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}\frac{1}{n!}T^{n}$
\end_inset

.
 First,
 we can see that 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{2}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
H^{2} & =\left(\sum_{j}E_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\right)\left(\sum_{n}E_{n}|E_{n}\rangle\langle E_{n}|\right)=\sum_{j,n}E_{j}E_{n}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|E_{n}\rangle\langle E_{n}|=\sum_{j}E^{2}_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where we used the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle E_{j}|E_{n}\right\rangle =\delta_{jn}$
\end_inset

.
 In general,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{n}=\sum_{j}E^{n}_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|$
\end_inset

.
 Writing then:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\exp(H) & =\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}\frac{1}{n!}H^{n}\\
 & =\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}\frac{1}{n!}\left(\sum_{j}E^{n}_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\right)\\
 & =\sum_{j}\left(\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}\frac{E^{n}_{j}}{n!}\right)|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And since 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(x)=\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}\frac{x^{n}}{n!}$
\end_inset

,
 then:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(H)=\sum_{j}e^{E_{j}}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|$
\end_inset

.
 In our case,
 we then have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t} & =i\hbar\sum_{j}\partial_{t}\exp\left(-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}\right)|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\\
 & =i\hbar\sum_{j}\left(-\frac{iE_{j}}{\hbar}\right)\exp\left(-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}\right)|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\\
 & =i\hbar\left(-\frac{i}{\hbar}\right)\sum_{j}\exp\left(-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}\right)E_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\\
 & =\sum_{j}e^{-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}}H|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}\\
 & =H\left(\sum_{j}e^{-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\right)
\end{align}

\end_inset

where we used 
\begin_inset Formula $H|E_{j}\rangle=E_{j}|E_{j}\rangle$
\end_inset

 throughout the entire proof.
 But now,
 since 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{j}e^{-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|=e^{-\frac{it}{\hbar}\sum_{j}E_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|}=e^{-\frac{iHt}{\hbar}|}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t}=He^{-\frac{iHt}{\hbar}|}$
\end_inset

 and since 
\begin_inset Formula $i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t}=H_{t}U_{t},$
\end_inset

 therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{t}=e^{-iHt/\hbar}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center

\series bold
Example:
 End
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
OBS.
 Note that we could obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{t}$
\end_inset

 by solving the Schrödinger equation for each Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

.
 However,
 having this general formula,
 we can begin the solution by calculating the exponential directly,
 without having to solve the differential equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now we can address another question related to “determinism” as opposed to free will.
 There are at least two senses of the word determinism.
 One is more closely associated with classical physics and consists of the idea that,
 if we know perfectly the current state of a system and the laws governing its evolution,
 its future and past states are,
 in principle,
 determined.
 The other is a broader sense,
 of a philosophical nature,
 which concerns the denial of free will.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Regarding the first sense,
 the usual formulation of quantum mechanics introduces a probabilistic element that contrasts with the determinism of classical mechanics.
 The results of measurements are,
 in general,
 not uniquely determined by the previous quantum state,
 but are described by probability distributions.
 In this sense,
 the theory contains an indeterministic element.
 It is worth noting,
 however,
 that the Schrödinger equation governing the evolution of the quantum state is deterministic.
 Given the initial state and the Hamiltonian of the system,
 the equation uniquely determines the quantum state at any subsequent time.
 The probabilities of measurement outcomes are then obtained from this state through the Born rule.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Whether nature is ultimately deterministic and the probabilistic nature of quantum mechanics results from some incomplete description,
 or whether quantum indeterminacy is fundamental,
 is a question for which we do not have a consensual answer.
 Different positions regarding this question give rise,
 in part,
 to different interpretations of quantum mechanics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 it is a mistake to think that this probabilistic mathematical element would,
 by itself,
 serve as support for a defense of free will.
 There is no formulation in the known laws of physics that incorporates free will.
 We can approximate the chance of obtaining heads or tails when tossing an approximately symmetric coin to 50% for each outcome.
 This is a probabilistic event,
 but we would not say,
 for that reason,
 that the coin possesses free will.
 The introduction of probabilities into the description of nature,
 therefore,
 does not confer any more free will upon nature than the probabilistic tossing of a coin.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore,
 not only is the evolution of the quantum state deterministic,
 but the probability distribution itself at any given instant imposes constraints on the possible outcomes and their probabilities.
 In my view,
 these constraints are incompatible with a conception of “free” will in which the agent should possess unrestricted freedom to determine any of the possible outcomes.
 
\end_layout

